55-12c8cb9eccb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-93951" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-t{y}^{′}+10y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-t{y}^{′}+10y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-4,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({c}_{1}+3{c}_{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+({c}_{2}-3{c}_{1})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=-4$ and ${c}_{1}=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2+3{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-93951/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-93951"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64855/selection/6bbc4885e55833d1813dca496804c8164f8cf9ea050d50406a04988f12550e69" :answer/id #uuid "0cab10d1-61f3-5e80-9cdf-696457eaddaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64855/selection/622d253e810c31bc0a0155923e84d519af74d18e89a1fbca345c7205895b1d22" :answer/id #uuid "c2cd4b37-fa3b-5286-b6d5-6c71aa6df67a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64855/selection/77aadcb9445a5344ec8c7845824f9f77a46584bf3835aaa01d120a2dc036ce42" :answer/id #uuid "0c48c095-93af-5c31-9e9a-73dd72fa3d77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64855/selection/8f75ea05d71c65bfbf58580aa12c20140c7d91c3ea1267b905f9fe15e780c881" :answer/id #uuid "53c3d573-0fed-5a6e-9ad9-02a0c47ae9d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64855/selection/e09695d0753689f55f3d44358f94722ac8df54655823e19f435c2c7d61806102" :answer/id #uuid "74e3ecbe-af5d-5e1c-8c0e-ed70ac225ea5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)=-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64855/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa68ca6f-07be-5c84-ad1f-1d8c842a341c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64855/selection/6bbc4885e55833d1813dca496804c8164f8cf9ea050d50406a04988f12550e69" :answer-field/choices ["q-64855/selection/6bbc4885e55833d1813dca496804c8164f8cf9ea050d50406a04988f12550e69" "q-64855/selection/622d253e810c31bc0a0155923e84d519af74d18e89a1fbca345c7205895b1d22" "q-64855/selection/77aadcb9445a5344ec8c7845824f9f77a46584bf3835aaa01d120a2dc036ce42" "q-64855/selection/8f75ea05d71c65bfbf58580aa12c20140c7d91c3ea1267b905f9fe15e780c881" "q-64855/selection/e09695d0753689f55f3d44358f94722ac8df54655823e19f435c2c7d61806102"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64855" :question/id #uuid "fcdb58b1-8227-5f77-80d1-bb3667b9970f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64855" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+20y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-4,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+20{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+20{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +20] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-2+4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{-2-4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{-2+4\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-2-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}+B\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{{c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-4.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=2$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2[(4+{c}_{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2-2{c}_{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2[2-2{c}_{2}] \\\\ -4 & =-4+4{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-64855/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64855"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6629"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=x^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=c_1 \\cos( 2 \\ln{x} )  + c_2\\sin( 2 \\ln{x} ).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6630"] :question/problem "Determine the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 y'' +5ty'+8y = 0,\\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=t^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+5t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +8] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t) = t^{-2} \\left( c_1 \\cos( 2 \\ln{t} ) + c_2 \\sin( 2 \\ln{t} ) \\right).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6631"] :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)= c_1 \\cos(7 \\ln{x} )  + c_2\\sin(7 \\ln{x} ),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1) = -7, \\quad x > 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0 = c_1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{7}{x}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -7$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =7{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = -\\sin(7 \\ln{x} ).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6632"] :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = t(c_1 \\cos( 3 \\ln{t} ) + c_2\\sin( 3 \\ln{t}) ),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0,  \\quad y'(1) = -1, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0  = c_1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -1$ and $c_1=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3{c}_{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & ={c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t) = -\\dfrac{ t\\sin( 3 \\ln{t}) )}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
